<header><p class="st-kicker">other · Scandinavia and the Baltic</p><h1 class="st-title">Governorate of Livonia</h1><p class="st-meta">1721 CE – 1918 CE · capital Riga</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Russian imperial province covering northern Latvia and southern Estonia, home to Riga and the University of Dorpat, where both Latvian and Estonian nationalism took shape.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> took <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/swedish-livonia">Swedish Livonia</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-northern-war">Great Northern War</a>; Riga fell in 1710, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-nystad">Treaty of Nystad</a> in 1721 confirmed the conquest. The new province, at first called the Riga <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/governorate">Governorate</a>, covered Latvian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vidzeme">Vidzeme</a>, southern <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/republic-of-estonia">Estonia</a> and, later, the island of Ösel.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The Livonian knighthood kept control of local government and the Lutheran church under the Baltic special order. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serfdom">Serfdom</a> was abolished in 1819. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/university-of-tartu">University of Dorpat</a>, reopened in 1802, became a centre of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baltic-germans">Baltic German</a> and later Estonian learning, and Riga grew into one of the empire's main industrial cities and ports. Latvian and Estonian national movements flourished, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russification">Russification</a> in the 1880s challenged German dominance.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In 1917 its northern part joined the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/autonomous-governorate-of-estonia">Autonomous Governorate of Estonia</a>. German forces occupied the rest in 1917-1918, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baltic-germans">Baltic Germans</a> planned the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/united-baltic-duchy">United Baltic Duchy</a>. After the war the territory was divided between the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/republic-of-latvia">Republic of Latvia</a> and the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/republic-of-estonia">Republic of Estonia</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/governorate-of-livonia.jpg" alt="Governorate of Livonia" width="320" height="189"><figcaption>Image: Milenioscuro · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Livonia_in_Russian_Empire_(1914).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1721 CE – 1918 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>other</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Riga</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Lutheran Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Latvian, Estonian, German, Russian</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/latvia">Latvia</a>, <a href="/countries/estonia">Estonia</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Livland Governorate, Riga Governorate, Livonia Governorate</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1820&amp;focus=governorate-of-livonia" aria-label="Governorate of Livonia on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5200 245 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Governorate of Livonia lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5520 501L5520 501M5520 501L6487 457M5520 501L5520 501"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5520 501h0M6487 457h0M5520 501h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5520 501h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5520 501h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Riga. <a href="/atlas?year=1820&amp;focus=governorate-of-livonia">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
